WEBVTT

00:00:00.043 --> 00:00:01.044 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Builder.

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.784 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Blueprint.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.074 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson one.

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:05.355 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Introduction.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.616 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Education only.

00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:09.489 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Not financial advice.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.061 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is general information and

00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:14.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
doesn't consider your objectives,

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
financial situation or needs.

00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:18.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto, a digital coin, is high risk;

00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:21.319 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you could lose all the money you put in.

00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:23.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past performance is not a reliable

00:00:23.904 --> 00:00:25.613 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
indicator of future results.

00:00:26.663 --> 00:00:29.056 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The prices are public historical prices

00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:31.234 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at the end of each day, marked Example.

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:34.266 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No order is sent from this lesson.

00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:37.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end of this series you will

00:00:37.164 --> 00:00:38.952 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
understand what a grid bot does.

00:00:39.349 --> 00:00:40.801 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is a small program that

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:42.366 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
places these orders for you.

00:00:42.822 --> 00:00:44.721 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will learn when it can capture

00:00:44.721 --> 00:00:46.285 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces, and where it loses.

00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:48.544 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will know how to plan one, test

00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:50.443 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
it on paper, run it, check it, and

00:00:50.443 --> 00:00:52.491 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
change the capital you give to bots

00:00:52.491 --> 00:00:54.352 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only under written risk rules.

00:00:54.872 --> 00:00:56.269 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is education, not

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:57.589 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a promise of results.

00:00:58.109 --> 00:00:59.719 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price often moves sideways,

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:01.469 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bouncing between the same top

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:03.220 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and bottom instead of leaving.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.450 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid is a ladder of waiting

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.159 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
orders, called limit orders,

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:08.646 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
built for that bouncing.

00:01:09.166 --> 00:01:11.010 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When price runs one way and stays

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.188 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
there, the grid has nothing left to do.

00:01:13.569 --> 00:01:15.322 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
And if price falls through the

00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:17.005 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, coin means the crypto

00:01:17.005 --> 00:01:18.828 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
itself, and you are left holding

00:01:18.828 --> 00:01:20.560 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the coin, and it is worth less.

00:01:21.491 --> 00:01:22.831 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end you will know

00:01:22.831 --> 00:01:24.116 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
what a grid bot places.

00:01:25.246 --> 00:01:27.324 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will see that idea on

00:01:27.324 --> 00:01:29.299 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
one real path from 2023.

00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:32.008 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will be able to say the

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:33.460 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stop rule in one sentence.

00:01:34.414 --> 00:01:36.260 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When a chart says Example, it

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.106 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
means a real past price used

00:01:38.106 --> 00:01:39.952 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
as an example, not a forecast.

00:01:41.002 --> 00:01:42.243 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto is a digital

00:01:42.243 --> 00:01:43.641 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
asset, such as bitcoin.

00:01:44.161 --> 00:01:45.837 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You do not need to have bought

00:01:45.837 --> 00:01:47.122 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
any before this lesson.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.467 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An exchange is the website or app

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:51.799 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
where you swap Australian dollars for

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:53.840 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crypto, and crypto back to dollars.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course does not name one,

00:01:56.036 --> 00:01:57.712 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it does not send an order.

00:01:58.457 --> 00:02:00.021 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Cash is money you can spend.

00:02:00.423 --> 00:02:02.259 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here that means dollars, or a

00:02:02.259 --> 00:02:04.334 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stablecoin, a crypto coin built

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:05.850 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
to stay at one US dollar.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.437 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We treat it like cash in this lesson,

00:02:08.437 --> 00:02:10.169 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
but it can drop below a dollar.

00:02:10.598 --> 00:02:12.789 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Coin is the crypto, such as bitcoin.

00:02:13.839 --> 00:02:16.186 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order is an instruction to buy or sell.

00:02:16.638 --> 00:02:18.445 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy order swaps cash for coin.

00:02:18.965 --> 00:02:20.868 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell order swaps coin back to

00:02:20.868 --> 00:02:22.999 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash, and you can only sell coin you

00:02:22.999 --> 00:02:24.902 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
already hold, on a spot account.

00:02:25.422 --> 00:02:27.058 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A spot account is the normal

00:02:27.058 --> 00:02:28.411 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
account on an exchange.

00:02:28.931 --> 00:02:30.327 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You own the coin you buy,

00:02:30.327 --> 00:02:31.668 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and nothing is borrowed.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.320 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot is software that places

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.942 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and refills orders for you.

00:02:36.462 --> 00:02:38.305 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You start with some cash and some

00:02:38.305 --> 00:02:39.981 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, so there is something to

00:02:39.981 --> 00:02:41.713 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy with and something to sell.

00:02:41.762 --> 00:02:44.114 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buy orders

00:02:44.114 --> 00:02:46.466 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on lines below the current price and

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:48.819 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell orders on lines above it, inside

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:50.622 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a top and a bottom you choose.

00:02:51.142 --> 00:02:52.818 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The space between your top and

00:02:52.818 --> 00:02:54.606 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your bottom is called the range.

00:02:54.699 --> 00:02:56.805 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sells need some coin, so a grid

00:02:56.805 --> 00:02:58.754 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
starts with part cash and part

00:02:58.754 --> 00:03:00.704 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, and that is a description,

00:03:00.704 --> 00:03:02.575 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not advice about what to hold.

00:03:03.625 --> 00:03:05.331 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A limit order is an order that

00:03:05.331 --> 00:03:06.895 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waits at a price you choose.

00:03:07.415 --> 00:03:08.979 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not chase the price.

00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:10.963 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Chasing the price would mean

00:03:10.963 --> 00:03:12.415 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
moving the order to follow

00:03:12.415 --> 00:03:13.980 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wherever the price has gone.

00:03:14.829 --> 00:03:16.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order fills when the price reaches

00:03:16.904 --> 00:03:18.636 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
its line and the trade happens.

00:03:19.097 --> 00:03:21.304 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Until then, the order is only waiting.

00:03:22.354 --> 00:03:24.309 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a ladder leaned on a wall.

00:03:24.451 --> 00:03:26.797 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The rungs wait, and they do not chase you.

00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:29.363 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A rung is one price where an order waits.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.949 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The gap is the space between two rungs.

00:03:32.238 --> 00:03:34.282 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The ladder is the analogy, and the

00:03:34.282 --> 00:03:36.554 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
chart beside it is the real past path.

00:03:37.604 --> 00:03:39.015 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top rung

00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:40.144 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the bottom rung.

00:03:40.664 --> 00:03:42.787 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the rungs between them.

00:03:43.136 --> 00:03:45.427 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is the written rule for leaving.

00:03:46.276 --> 00:03:48.119 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top line and the

00:03:48.119 --> 00:03:49.963 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom line, taken from the path.

00:03:50.442 --> 00:03:51.930 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Hindsight: this range is

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.195 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
drawn after the fact.

00:03:53.715 --> 00:03:56.939 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The chart is bitcoin's real daily

00:03:56.939 --> 00:04:00.509 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes from 19 July to 16 October 2023.

00:04:01.029 --> 00:04:02.481 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Every close in this window

00:04:02.481 --> 00:04:04.213 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stayed between those two lines.

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:07.405 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Six gaps of about 900 dollars each sit

00:04:07.405 --> 00:04:09.966 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
between the bottom line and the top line.

00:04:10.486 --> 00:04:12.713 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each gap is about 900 dollars:

00:04:12.713 --> 00:04:14.940 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the top line minus the bottom

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.424 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
line, divided by six.

00:04:16.944 --> 00:04:18.289 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is one step between

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:19.858 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines, not the whole range.

00:04:20.378 --> 00:04:22.217 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Seven lines make those six gaps.

00:04:22.737 --> 00:04:24.525 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the prices on the

00:04:24.525 --> 00:04:26.201 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
rungs where limit orders wait.

00:04:27.187 --> 00:04:28.545 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Buy orders wait on lines

00:04:28.545 --> 00:04:29.903 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
below the current price.

00:04:30.423 --> 00:04:32.379 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Sell orders wait on lines above it.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.359 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fill changes what you hold: a buy

00:04:35.359 --> 00:04:38.025 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fill adds coin, and a sell fill adds cash.

00:04:38.545 --> 00:04:39.718 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You choose the range,

00:04:39.718 --> 00:04:41.003 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the gaps and the money.

00:04:41.312 --> 00:04:42.765 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot places each order.

00:04:43.771 --> 00:04:45.621 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
After a fill, the bot places the

00:04:45.621 --> 00:04:47.694 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
opposite order one rung up or down.

00:04:48.214 --> 00:04:50.392 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy fill gets a sell one rung higher.

00:04:50.779 --> 00:04:53.008 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell fill gets a buy one rung lower.

00:04:53.528 --> 00:04:55.148 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You still write the stop, and

00:04:55.148 --> 00:04:56.824 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you act when that rule is met.

00:04:57.056 --> 00:04:59.291 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop for you

00:04:59.291 --> 00:05:01.414 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on, and

00:05:01.414 --> 00:05:03.481 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you write which one before you start.

00:05:04.215 --> 00:05:05.556 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a market stall.

00:05:05.988 --> 00:05:08.102 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stallholder buys stock a little

00:05:08.102 --> 00:05:10.216 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cheaper and sells it a little dearer,

00:05:10.216 --> 00:05:11.836 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and pays a fee on every sale.

00:05:12.286 --> 00:05:13.856 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Exchanges charge a small

00:05:13.856 --> 00:05:14.902 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fee on each trade.

00:05:15.422 --> 00:05:17.165 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid can do that while price

00:05:17.165 --> 00:05:18.691 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces inside the range.

00:05:19.211 --> 00:05:21.732 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bounce means price moves down and back

00:05:21.732 --> 00:05:24.101 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
up, or up and back down, and stays inside.

00:05:24.621 --> 00:05:26.857 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One buy at a lower line and the later

00:05:26.857 --> 00:05:28.940 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell of that same coin is one pair.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.640 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On these daily closes, the July to

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.900 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
October example window finished

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.000 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only three pairs, each a buy and a

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.099 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell on lines next to each other.

00:05:38.619 --> 00:05:40.351 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sell line is higher, so the

00:05:40.351 --> 00:05:42.195 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash that comes back is more than

00:05:42.195 --> 00:05:44.150 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the cash that left, before the fee.

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:45.896 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A fee is what the exchange

00:05:45.896 --> 00:05:47.460 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
charges when an order fills.

00:05:47.641 --> 00:05:49.931 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:05:49.993 --> 00:05:51.501 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the fees are larger than

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:53.121 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that, the pair keeps nothing.

00:05:53.295 --> 00:05:54.538 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is not a promise.

00:05:55.058 --> 00:05:56.862 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair finishes only if price

00:05:56.862 --> 00:05:58.735 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
does bounce and both orders fill.

00:05:59.785 --> 00:06:01.014 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The daily close is the

00:06:01.014 --> 00:06:02.243 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
last price of the day.

00:06:02.664 --> 00:06:04.464 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto trades all day, so the

00:06:04.464 --> 00:06:05.872 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
day is cut at a set time.

00:06:06.392 --> 00:06:08.714 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This lesson uses the end of each

00:06:08.714 --> 00:06:10.857 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
UTC day, the world's standard

00:06:10.857 --> 00:06:13.358 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
clock, which is 11am Sydney time in

00:06:13.358 --> 00:06:15.769 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daylight saving, 10am otherwise.

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:18.273 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a simple candle that shows

00:06:18.273 --> 00:06:19.949 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the day's high, low and close.

00:06:20.422 --> 00:06:22.352 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Traders call it a candle because

00:06:22.352 --> 00:06:24.281 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the full version has a thick body

00:06:24.281 --> 00:06:26.013 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and a thin wick, like a candle.

00:06:26.445 --> 00:06:27.953 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we only need the high,

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:29.182 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the low and the close.

00:06:29.516 --> 00:06:32.695 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This candle is 19 July 2023.

00:06:33.215 --> 00:06:34.444 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high, the low, and

00:06:34.444 --> 00:06:35.729 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the close are labelled.

00:06:36.622 --> 00:06:38.751 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The example is Bitcoin's public

00:06:38.751 --> 00:06:40.407 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily price in US dollars.

00:06:40.927 --> 00:06:44.105 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
From 19 July 2023 to 16 October

00:06:44.105 --> 00:06:47.028 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023, every one of those days

00:06:47.028 --> 00:06:49.570 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closed inside the range.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:51.763 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The close crossed the seven

00:06:51.763 --> 00:06:53.509 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines 28 times in that window.

00:06:54.029 --> 00:06:56.341 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The count is an example, not a forecast.

00:06:56.861 --> 00:06:58.649 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We do not need to read each day.

00:06:58.915 --> 00:07:00.257 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The shape is the lesson,

00:07:00.257 --> 00:07:01.671 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it is marked Example.

00:07:02.191 --> 00:07:04.947 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Worked check: 19 July

00:07:04.947 --> 00:07:08.027 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023 to 16 October 2023.

00:07:08.547 --> 00:07:10.056 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The low is the bottom line.

00:07:10.536 --> 00:07:12.002 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high is the top line.

00:07:12.522 --> 00:07:14.366 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Nothing on this card is an order.

00:07:15.329 --> 00:07:18.172 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Those 28 crossings, each time the price

00:07:18.172 --> 00:07:20.571 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crosses a line, are not 28 results.

00:07:21.091 --> 00:07:23.293 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A crossing only means the close moved

00:07:23.293 --> 00:07:25.360 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
from one side of a line to the other.

00:07:25.802 --> 00:07:27.513 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A pair is finished only when a

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:29.367 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy and its later sell have both

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:31.099 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled, and only after the fee.

00:07:31.599 --> 00:07:33.498 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The price can cross many lines and

00:07:33.498 --> 00:07:35.397 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
still leave you holding coin if it

00:07:35.397 --> 00:07:37.018 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
walks out through the bottom.

00:07:37.554 --> 00:07:39.062 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You can buy part of a coin.

00:07:39.611 --> 00:07:42.571 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we buy one hundredth

00:07:42.612 --> 00:07:43.673 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
of a bitcoin, 0.01.

00:07:43.673 --> 00:07:46.936 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
900 dollars times 0.01 bitcoin

00:07:46.936 --> 00:07:49.459 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is 9 dollars before fees.

00:07:49.979 --> 00:07:52.960 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In this example a buy waiting at the

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.250 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower of these two lines was reached on

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.231 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
17 August 2023, and the sell one line

00:07:59.231 --> 00:08:02.212 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above was reached on 29 August 2023.

00:08:02.732 --> 00:08:04.520 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The card shows the gap, then the

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.084 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
two fees, then what is left.

00:08:06.393 --> 00:08:09.599 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fee is 0.1 percent of the whole

00:08:09.599 --> 00:08:12.474 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
trade, not of the profit: about 27

00:08:12.474 --> 00:08:15.238 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cents on the 267 dollar buy and

00:08:15.238 --> 00:08:18.113 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
about 28 cents on the 276 dollar

00:08:18.113 --> 00:08:20.766 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell, about 54 cents together.

00:08:21.286 --> 00:08:23.111 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is about 8 dollars 46

00:08:23.111 --> 00:08:25.196 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
after fees, for one small pair.

00:08:25.716 --> 00:08:27.615 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It happens only if the price comes

00:08:27.615 --> 00:08:29.291 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
back up through the sell line.

00:08:29.396 --> 00:08:31.127 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the price leaves through the

00:08:31.127 --> 00:08:32.915 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, you still hold the coin,

00:08:32.915 --> 00:08:34.479 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the pair never finishes.

00:08:34.671 --> 00:08:36.459 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This card is not money you made.

00:08:36.857 --> 00:08:39.203 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past results don't predict future results.

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:47.979 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Now watch what you hold when the

00:08:47.979 --> 00:08:50.046 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
price leaves the range and stays out.

00:08:50.426 --> 00:08:52.102 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Above the range the sells have

00:08:52.102 --> 00:08:53.890 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding cash,

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.342 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with nothing left to sell.

00:08:55.664 --> 00:08:57.284 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Below the range the buys have

00:08:57.284 --> 00:08:58.737 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding

00:08:58.737 --> 00:09:00.469 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, with nothing left to buy.

00:09:00.846 --> 00:09:02.238 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is why the way down

00:09:02.238 --> 00:09:03.996 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
matters most: every buy fills,

00:09:03.996 --> 00:09:05.755 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you are left holding coin.

00:09:07.805 --> 00:09:10.039 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here is that stop rule on the real path.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.805 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It triggers if a daily close is outside

00:09:12.805 --> 00:09:15.150 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the range, through the top or the bottom,

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.641 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the next daily close is still outside.

00:09:18.161 --> 00:09:19.614 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then the idea is finished.

00:09:19.994 --> 00:09:22.088 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot, which cancels the

00:09:22.088 --> 00:09:23.957 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waiting orders, and do what you

00:09:23.957 --> 00:09:26.051 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wrote: keep the coin or swap to cash.

00:09:26.571 --> 00:09:28.553 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot's automatic stop is a price

00:09:28.553 --> 00:09:30.241 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that can fire during the day.

00:09:30.761 --> 00:09:32.460 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course uses the two daily

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.296 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes, unless your written plan

00:09:34.296 --> 00:09:36.083 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
says you turned on a price stop.

00:09:36.515 --> 00:09:37.856 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a teaching rule,

00:09:37.856 --> 00:09:39.034 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not a recommendation.

00:09:39.554 --> 00:09:41.174 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is your written rule

00:09:41.174 --> 00:09:42.627 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
for when you stop the bot.

00:09:42.859 --> 00:09:44.993 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On 23 October 2023 the close

00:09:44.993 --> 00:09:47.405 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
finished above the top line, and

00:09:47.405 --> 00:09:49.724 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day was still above it.

00:09:54.774 --> 00:09:56.227 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the top, the sells

00:09:56.227 --> 00:09:57.903 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
have filled and you hold cash.

00:09:58.012 --> 00:09:59.855 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the bottom, the buys have

00:09:59.855 --> 00:10:01.699 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you hold coin that you

00:10:01.699 --> 00:10:03.710 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bought above where the price is now.

00:10:04.069 --> 00:10:05.521 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That loss can keep growing

00:10:05.521 --> 00:10:06.639 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
until the stop acts.

00:10:06.944 --> 00:10:08.787 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot either way, then

00:10:08.787 --> 00:10:10.352 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you follow the written rule.

00:10:10.461 --> 00:10:12.248 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop

00:10:12.248 --> 00:10:14.148 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:15.842 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
EXAMPLE, made up: three

00:10:15.842 --> 00:10:17.422 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buys fill on the way down.

00:10:17.942 --> 00:10:21.241 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth of a bitcoin

00:10:21.241 --> 00:10:24.541 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at 27,630 is 276 dollars 30.

00:10:25.395 --> 00:10:30.929 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 26,730 is 267 dollars 30.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:36.962 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 25,830 is 258 dollars 30.

00:10:37.482 --> 00:10:40.122 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Together that is 801 dollars 90.

00:10:40.885 --> 00:10:45.092 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Three buys of 0.01 is 0.03 bitcoin.

00:10:45.716 --> 00:10:48.902 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
At 24,000, that is 720 dollars.

00:10:49.422 --> 00:10:51.689 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A little below the range, at a

00:10:51.689 --> 00:10:54.054 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
made-up price of 24,000, that

00:10:54.054 --> 00:10:56.124 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin is worth 720 dollars.

00:10:56.644 --> 00:10:58.270 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You are down 81 dollars 90

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:00.206 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
before fees, and the loss keeps

00:11:00.206 --> 00:11:02.064 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
growing until your stop acts.

00:11:03.114 --> 00:11:04.689 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A common mistake is thinking

00:11:04.689 --> 00:11:06.198 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot predicts the price.

00:11:06.986 --> 00:11:07.736 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not.

00:11:08.302 --> 00:11:09.085 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It only waits.

00:11:09.454 --> 00:11:10.963 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Another mistake is starting

00:11:10.963 --> 00:11:12.136 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with no written stop.

00:11:12.551 --> 00:11:13.780 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
There is no promise of

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:15.065 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
results in this lesson.

00:11:15.256 --> 00:11:16.863 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Numbers marked Example are

00:11:16.863 --> 00:11:18.541 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
teaching numbers, not advice.

00:11:19.591 --> 00:11:21.855 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper or demo trading is like driving

00:11:21.855 --> 00:11:23.827 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with learner plates, or training

00:11:23.827 --> 00:11:25.871 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wheels: you practise with pretend

00:11:25.871 --> 00:11:27.697 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
money before anything is real.

00:11:28.217 --> 00:11:29.734 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson 7 shows you how.

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:31.918 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps one to six.

00:11:32.618 --> 00:11:34.306 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This introduction is step one.

00:11:35.006 --> 00:11:36.067 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then what you need.

00:11:36.697 --> 00:11:38.224 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then exactly what a grid

00:11:38.224 --> 00:11:39.675 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot does, order by order.

00:11:40.675 --> 00:11:41.792 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then market regimes.

00:11:42.369 --> 00:11:44.436 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then choosing grid spacing and range.

00:11:45.075 --> 00:11:46.681 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then entry and exit rules.

00:11:47.397 --> 00:11:48.697 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps seven to eleven.

00:11:49.397 --> 00:11:50.481 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then a paper test.

00:11:51.201 --> 00:11:52.263 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then weekly checks.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.189 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then common mistakes.

00:11:54.889 --> 00:11:56.900 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then scaling up under written rules.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:58.751 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then an opt-in lesson on

00:11:58.751 --> 00:12:00.837 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
liquidation and your complete plan.

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:03.786 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The next lesson is a checklist you

00:12:03.786 --> 00:12:05.686 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
write on paper, before any screen.

00:12:06.707 --> 00:12:09.038 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In short: a grid waits, it buys lower and

00:12:09.038 --> 00:12:11.669 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sells higher while price bounces, the fees

00:12:11.669 --> 00:12:13.999 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
come out of what the pair makes, and if a

00:12:13.999 --> 00:12:16.555 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily close leaves and stays out, you stop

00:12:16.555 --> 00:12:18.810 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:12:19.894 --> 00:12:20.760 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Check yourself.

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:22.733 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Why does a grid start with

00:12:22.733 --> 00:12:24.185 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
some coin as well as cash?

00:12:24.349 --> 00:12:25.633 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Because the sell orders

00:12:25.633 --> 00:12:27.142 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
need coin you already hold.

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.007 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example pair, why is the net about

00:12:31.007 --> 00:12:33.766 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
8 dollars 46 and not the full 9 dollars?

00:12:34.286 --> 00:12:35.850 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The two fees come out first.

00:12:37.012 --> 00:12:39.079 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price closes above your top line, and

00:12:39.079 --> 00:12:41.258 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day's close is still above it.

00:12:41.566 --> 00:12:43.354 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
What do you do, and who does it?

00:12:43.674 --> 00:12:45.462 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot and follow your

00:12:45.462 --> 00:12:47.305 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
written rule, by hand, unless you

00:12:47.305 --> 00:12:49.149 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
turned on the bot's stop setting.

00:12:50.206 --> 00:12:50.956 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Your turn.

00:12:51.402 --> 00:12:53.134 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On paper, write the top and the

00:12:53.134 --> 00:12:55.089 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, one gap, and the stop rule.

00:12:56.193 --> 00:12:57.924 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Say what you will do if a daily

00:12:57.924 --> 00:12:59.600 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
close leaves the range and the

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:01.165 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside.

00:13:01.539 --> 00:13:02.936 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Pause the video and write

00:13:02.936 --> 00:13:04.556 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your answer before you go on.

00:13:05.589 --> 00:13:07.656 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No real order is sent in this lesson.

00:13:08.143 --> 00:13:10.046 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper practice comes in Lesson

00:13:10.046 --> 00:13:11.656 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
7, and real money only after

00:13:11.656 --> 00:13:13.486 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your written graduation rule.

00:13:14.006 --> 00:13:16.013 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A graduation rule is the written

00:13:16.013 --> 00:13:17.722 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
test you pass on paper first.

00:13:18.255 --> 00:13:19.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You draw the idea.

00:13:19.784 --> 00:13:21.578 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a screen appears later, it

00:13:21.578 --> 00:13:23.293 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
should be a copy of the page,

00:13:23.293 --> 00:13:24.853 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and only on paper or demo.

00:13:26.053 --> 00:13:28.414 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring a notebook, and no secrets: no

00:13:28.414 --> 00:13:31.028 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
seed phrase, private key or API secret.

00:13:31.548 --> 00:13:33.657 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A seed phrase or private key is the

00:13:33.657 --> 00:13:35.841 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
master password to a crypto wallet.

00:13:36.361 --> 00:13:38.316 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An API secret is a key that lets an

00:13:38.316 --> 00:13:40.259 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
app place trades on your account,

00:13:40.259 --> 00:13:42.281 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and sometimes move money out of it.

00:13:43.331 --> 00:13:45.023 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair, in the next lesson,

00:13:45.023 --> 00:13:46.638 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is the two things you swap,

00:13:46.638 --> 00:13:48.407 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
like bitcoin and US dollars.

00:13:49.457 --> 00:13:51.698 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buys below

00:13:51.698 --> 00:13:53.939 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the current price and waiting sells

00:13:53.939 --> 00:13:56.030 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above it, inside a range you choose.

00:13:57.230 --> 00:13:58.348 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A finished pair buys

00:13:58.348 --> 00:13:59.633 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower and sells higher.

00:13:59.839 --> 00:14:02.130 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:14:02.191 --> 00:14:04.249 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example that was about 8

00:14:04.249 --> 00:14:06.471 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
dollars 46, and it is not a promise.

00:14:07.671 --> 00:14:09.068 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the top and

00:14:09.068 --> 00:14:10.241 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you hold mostly cash.

00:14:10.691 --> 00:14:12.031 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the bottom

00:14:12.031 --> 00:14:13.428 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you hold mostly coin.

00:14:14.469 --> 00:14:16.778 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a daily close leaves the range and the

00:14:16.778 --> 00:14:19.088 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside, you stop the

00:14:19.088 --> 00:14:20.987 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:14:22.177 --> 00:14:24.412 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Next lesson: What you are going to need.

00:14:24.801 --> 00:14:26.421 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring your notes: your range,

00:14:26.421 --> 00:14:28.041 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
